###  http://example.org/exp_aa002-water-pcseg2
<http://example.org/exp_aa002-water-pcseg2> rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                                                     <http://purl.org/gc/MolecularComputation> ,
                                                     <http://purl.org/gc/SinglePoint> ;
                                            <http://purl.org/gc/hasResult> <http://example.org/energies_aa002-water-pcseg2> ;
                                            <http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#generated> <http://example.org/file_aa002-water-pcseg2_dat> ,
                                                                                  <http://example.org/file_aa002-water-pcseg2_log> ;
                                            <http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#used> <http://example.org/file_aa002-water-pcseg2_inp> ;
                                            <http://purl.org/gc/hasBasisSet> "PCSEG-2" ;
                                            <http://purl.org/gc/hasMethod> "wB97X-D" ;
                                            <http://example.org/hasRuntyp> "ENERGY" ;
                                            <http://example.org/hasSolvationModel> "PCM" ;
                                            <http://example.org/hasSolvent> "water" ;
                                            rdfs:label "Single point aa002-water-pcseg2" ;
                                            <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#editorialNote> "single-point energy at wB97X-D/PCseg-2/PCM(water) for standalone water; -76.4501868289 Hartree. Combined with 6-21G+(d,p) thermal data: dG_hyd = -0.74 kcal/mol (0K, elec+ZPE), -2.35 kcal/mol (dH, 298K), but +9.42 kcal/mol (dG, 298K) -- badly wrong vs experimental -0.11 kcal/mol. -TdS = +11.8 kcal/mol, remarkably close to chloroacetaldehyde's own +11.9 kcal/mol artifact (see caa run_notes) -- strong, reproducible evidence the standalone water's ideal-gas translational entropy is systematically too high. Applying the standard 1 atm -> 1M standard-state correction (-1.89 kcal/mol) or 1 atm -> 55.5M (-4.27 kcal/mol) helps but does not fully close the gap to experiment, suggesting additional error from the basis/functional combination itself; enthalpy remains the more trustworthy comparison point for now" .


